title: Counseling before androgen deprivation therapy
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Before starting:'' Explain the indication, expected benefit, planned duration, and dosing/adherence schedule for androgen deprivation therapy (ADT). Arrange [[monitoring|Monitoring during androgen deprivation therapy]] and primary-care coordination before the first dose.
* ''Expected effects:'' Discuss hot flashes, reduced libido/erections, genital shrinkage, fatigue, muscle loss, weight gain, anemia, breast symptoms, mood changes, and possible cognitive complaints. Explain increased fracture, diabetes, and cardiovascular risk. Testosterone recovery after stopping is variable and may be incomplete, especially after prolonged treatment; orchiectomy is permanent.
* ''Prevention:'' Start aerobic plus resistance exercise, preferably supervised; add balance training and adapt activity to skeletal metastases/fall risk. Encourage healthy weight/diet, smoking cessation, and limited alcohol. Optimize hypertension, diabetes, and lipids; involve cardiology for established cardiovascular disease.
* ''Bone health:'' Aim for calcium 1,000–1,200 mg/day from food plus supplements if needed; correct vitamin D deficiency. Supplements alone do not replace indicated [[Antiresorptive therapy]].
* ''Symptom support:'' Invite discussion of sexuality with the partner when desired; offer sexual-health and psychosocial support. For troublesome hot flashes, consider off-label nonhormonal treatment such as gabapentin. Erectile treatments may not restore libido.
* ''Flare/urgent symptoms:'' Gonadotropin-releasing hormone agonists initially raise testosterone; select an antagonist or appropriate antiandrogen protection when flare is hazardous. New severe back pain, leg weakness, saddle numbness, or urinary retention warrants urgent assessment.

Sources: NCCN Prostate Cancer v7.2026, PROS-G 6, PROS-B 1–3; ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 14, pp. 761–762 (PDF 768–769), ch. 7, Hot Flashes (PDF 376); [[EAU 2026, §8.2.5/8.3.2|https://uroweb.org/guidelines/prostate-cancer/chapter/quality-of-life-outcomes-in-prostate-cancer]].
